Fixed #1209 and committed it. bash -c "kill -9 1", sh -c 'crontab -r' and the other -c spellings now get the same verdict as the bare command. fmt, clippy with -D warnings and the full cargo test all pass.

What changed (`src/bash_parse.rs`): a new helper, dash_c_body, finds the -c string passed to bash, sh, zsh, dash or ksh. It looks past wrappers like sudo, env and xargs, and handles flag clusters like -lc/-ec, -o value and --. When the string is literal, the whole safety analysis runs on it, the same as if it had been typed on its own. Nested -c calls are followed up to 8 levels deep; anything deeper asks for confirmation. A literal body that won't parse, or that runs eval, also asks for confirmation.

Before and after. Both tables are posted on #1209: the "before" went up before the first code edit, the "after" right after the re-probe.
• Before: 15 of the 33 safety checks with a test trigger refused the bare command but let all five -c spellings through. The issue said 5, so the gap was three times wider than filed.
• After: 0 of 33.
• Wider sweep: I compared every verdict before and after over 4,440 commands. 317 went from allowed to refused, and every one is a literal -c body. None went toward allowed.
• Newly sent to confirmation: bash -c 'eval kill' and sh -c 'echo $(('. Both were previously auto-approved under an allow = ["bash *"] rule.

Recall control: with the new branch disabled (marker counted as 1 first), the new census test failed by name; restored, it passed. It ran as one command.

Not changed, on purpose: a body built at run time can't be read ahead of time, so it is handled exactly as before.
• bash -c "$CMD" already asked for confirmation.
• sh -c "kill -9 $PID" still passes, but so does the bare kill -9 $PID. That's not a -c gap, so I didn't file a new issue.

Small side changes:
• trigger_per_check in the safety tests, and the module holding it, are now pub(crate), so the new census reuses the same trigger table.
• docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md now explains the -c handling, and its dead (#1196) link points to the open #1210.
• I added a dated note to the bash_parse.rs entry in ARCHITECTURE.md.

The GitHub token expired partway through. I refreshed it in the same way evolve.sh does, without touching the git remote, then deleted the temporary token file.

The commit is ready but not pushed; Fixes #1209 will close the issue when it is pushed.

